<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cao Pi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">187–226 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cao Pi was the son of the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> and the first emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Wei dynasty</a>. In 220 CE he deposed the last Han emperor and declared his own dynasty. The act formally ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> and opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cao-pi.jpg" alt="Cao Pi" width="306" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yan Li-pen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cao_Pi_Tang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cao Pi was the second son of the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, but the eldest of the sons of Cao Cao's concubine, later wife, Lady Bian; the eldest of all, Cao Ang, died early. In 211 he became General of the Household for All Purposes and Vice Imperial Chancellor, second only to his father.</p><p>For years it was unclear whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> intended as heir. He favoured his third son Cao Zhi, known for his literary talent, and in 216 Cao Zhi's party falsely accused two officials who supported Cao Pi, Cui Yan and Mao Jie; Cui Yan was executed. In November or December 217 Cao Cao, by then King of Wei, named Cao Pi heir apparent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> died in spring 220, Cao Pi declared himself King of Wei before Emperor Xian had confirmed it, and sent his brothers, Cao Zhang and Cao Zhi among them, back to their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>. In the winter of 220 he pressed Emperor Xian to yield the throne. He declined three times, a pattern later usurpers in Chinese history followed, and then accepted, founding the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a>. This ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> briefly submitted to Wei, and Cao Pi rejected the strategist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-ye">Liu Ye</a>'s advice to attack him, granting him the title King of Wu instead; most historians believe this choice confined Wei to northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-xiaoting">Battle of Xiaoting</a> in 222 Sun Quan broke with Wei and founded Eastern Wu, and Cao Pi's attacks over the next years made no significant advances. In 225 he remarked that Heaven created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> to divide the north and the south.</p><p>Historians view him as a competent but unspectacular administrator. He formally established Chen Qun's nine-rank system as the basis of civil service nomination, but he reduced the princes' power and restricted their use of military force. He named Cao Rui crown prince only in summer 226, when seriously ill, and entrusted him to Cao Zhen, Chen Qun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-yi">Sima Yi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Cao Pi was a poet and scholar like his father and his brother Cao Zhi. His Yan Ge Xing is the first Chinese poem with seven syllables to the line. He also wrote over a hundred articles, and the collection of strange tales Lieyi Zhuan is attributed to him. After his death Cao Rui gave him the posthumous name Emperor Wen and the temple name Gaozu.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cao Pi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dynasty ended in 220 CE when his son <b>Cao Pi</b> deposed Emperor Xian and declared the Wei dynasty, opening the Three Kingdoms period before China was briefly reunified under the Jin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Xian, the last emperor of the Eastern Han dynasty and long a puppet of warlords, was forced to abdicate by <b>Cao Pi</b>, son of Cao Cao.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-11">11 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in early 220 his son <b>Cao Pi</b> forced Emperor Xian to abdicate and proclaimed the Wei dynasty, with its capital at Luoyang.</blockquote><a href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Cao Pi</b> ended the Han in 220, Liu Bei proclaimed himself emperor at Chengdu in 221, presenting his state as the legitimate Han.</blockquote><a href="/state/shu-han">Shu Han</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bozhou, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.86,115.77&amp;year=212" aria-label="Bozhou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7502 936 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cao Pi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7952 1206h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7952 1206h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.86,115.77&amp;year=212">What was here in 212 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Alas that I was born in these times, To be blown along like a cloud puffed by the wind!&#8221;</p><footer>He abandons the campaign &#183; tr. Arthur Waley, 1918 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42290/pg42290-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;To sit idle is not my desire: Gladly enough would I go to my country’s aid.&#8221;</p><footer>The campaign against Wu &#183; tr. Arthur Waley, 1918 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42290/pg42290-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
